<HelpChapterSection Id="roles">
    <HelpTopicSection Id="roles-permissions">
        <p>A permission is the right to perform a specific action. Below are all permissions of the cabinet, grouped by module. Your own permissions are marked <Icon Name="circle-check" />.</p>
        @foreach (var module in ModuleGroups)
        {
            <h4><Icon Name="@Display.IconOf(module.Key)" /> @Display.Text(module.Key)</h4>
            <div class="help-table-wrap">
                <table class="help-table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Permission</th><th>What it allows</th><th>You have it</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var permission in module)
                        {
                            <tr>
                                <td>@T(permission.Title)</td>
                                <td>@T(permission.Description)</td>
                                <td>@if (CurrentUser.Has(permission.Code)) { <Icon Name="circle-check" Title="Yes" /> } else { <span class="ds-muted">—</span> }</td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        }
        <p>Several document permissions determine <strong>whose</strong> documents you see — see “<a href="/help#documents-visibility">Whose documents you see</a>”.</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="roles-system">
        <p>When KOD is deployed, it creates standard roles common to most organizations. The system administrator can change their names and permissions and create custom roles for their domain — the current set of roles in your cabinet is shown in <span class="help-ui">Administration</span> → <span class="help-ui">Roles</span> (if you have access).</p>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
            <table class="help-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Default permissions</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var role in KOD.Domain.Access.SystemRoles.All)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>@T(role.Name)</td>
                            <td>@T(role.Description)</td>
                            <td>@string.Join(", ", role.Permissions.Select(TitleOf))</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>The <strong>“@AdministratorName”</strong> role always has all permissions, including new ones added in future versions.</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>
</HelpChapterSection>
